@node Obtaining Emacsvox
@section Obtaining Emacsvox

Before beginning@comma{} install @code{git}@comma{} @code{make}@comma{} GNU Texinfo (which
provides @code{makeinfo})@comma{} and
@uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/download.html, Emacs 31 or newer}.
The Speech Backends chapter lists the additional platform prerequisites for
the recommended Omnivox server.

The maintained source is
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/emacsvox}.  Clone it into the permanent
directory where it will be used:

@example
git clone https://github.com/bartbunting/emacsvox
cd emacsvox
@end example

The checkout itself is the source-checkout installation.  The build operates
in place; it does not create a second directory to copy elsewhere.

@node Quick Installation
@section First Speech Checklist

@enumerate
@item
Select Emacs 31 or newer.  If it is not the system @code{emacs}@comma{} copy
@code{local.mk.example} to the ignored @code{local.mk} and set @code{EMACS}:

@example
EMACS=/path/to/emacs-31/bin/emacs
@end example

Verify the selection before diagnosing a compiler failure:

@example
make check-emacs
@end example

@item
Configure and compile the checkout:

@example
make all
@end example

Do not compile Emacsvox with an older system Emacs.  Tests prefer source and
therefore do not prove that the byte-code used by a normal startup is current.

@item
Install the recommended Omnivox speech server.  @xref{Speech Backends}@comma{} for
the supported platform matrix@comma{} exact prerequisite classes@comma{} source and WSL2
build paths@comma{} and compatibility alternatives.

@item
Run the non-audible launcher preflight from the Emacsvox checkout:

@example
./bin/emacsvox --diagnose
@end example

It validates the checkout@comma{} Emacs@comma{} and backend without playing audio@comma{} creating
an Omnivox session log@comma{} or opening interactive Emacs.  Before continuing@comma{}
confirm that its report includes Emacs 31 or newer@comma{} the @code{omnivox} speech
backend@comma{} and an isolated @code{-Q} startup.  Correct any path or version
error that it prints.

@item
Run the deliberately audible backend check:

@example
./bin/emacsvox --check
@end example

The launcher prints its selections and explicitly announces that the audible
check is starting.  Continue only after hearing both the tone and speech.  Do
not diagnose Emacs configuration until this standalone Omnivox check passes.

@item
Prove a clean Emacsvox start:

@example
./bin/emacsvox
@end example

The launcher defaults to Omnivox@comma{} uses the selected Emacs 31 or newer@comma{} and
loads the setup source by its absolute path.  The session uses @code{-Q}: it
loads neither site files nor a personal Emacs init file.  This makes first
speech a reproducible installation proof rather than a side effect of
existing configuration.

@code{./bin/emacsvox --diagnose} prints the selected root@comma{} setup source@comma{}
Emacs version@comma{} and backend without playing audio or starting interactive
Emacs.  @code{./bin/emacsvox --help} documents explicit @code{EMACS}@comma{}
@code{TTS_PROGRAM}@comma{} and @code{EMACSVOX_DIR} overrides.  Arguments after
@code{--} are passed to Emacs.
@end enumerate

@node Confirming First Speech
@section Confirming First Speech

A successful isolated start speaks a versioned introduction ending with:

@quotation
I am completely operational@comma{} and all my circuits are functioning perfectly!

@end quotation

The preceding overview key and version number can change.  The sentence above
is the stable first-speech checkpoint.  A startup auditory icon may also play@comma{}
but it depends on optional audio support and is not part of acceptance.

Before changing personal configuration@comma{} make these checks in the isolated
session:

@enumerate
@item
Type @kbd{M-x tts-speak-version RET}.  The live speech adapter should speak its
version response.

@item
Type @kbd{C-h v tts-program RET}.  The displayed current value should name
@code{omnivox}.

@item
Use @kbd{C-n} and @kbd{C-p} to move over lines and confirm ordinary spoken
navigation.  Type @kbd{M-x emacsvox-describe-emacsvox RET} for the Emacsvox
overview.  @xref{Basic Usage}@comma{} for the tested starter commands.
@end enumerate

Only after these checks pass is the isolated first-use proof complete.

@node Recovering From No Speech
@section Recovering From No Speech

Do not add Emacsvox to a personal init file while recovering.  Start at the
first failing boundary:

@table @asis
@item @code{./bin/emacsvox --diagnose} fails
No audio or interactive Emacs has started.  Follow the reported selection:
set @code{EMACS}@comma{} correct @code{EMACS=} in @code{local.mk}@comma{} or install Emacs 31
or newer when the Emacs check fails.  When the speech backend is not
executable@comma{} finish installing Omnivox@comma{} put it on @code{PATH}@comma{} or set
@code{OMNIVOX_PROGRAM} to its absolute executable.  Under WSL2@comma{} rebuild or
verify the staged runtime described in @ref{Building Omnivox For WSL2}.
@item The diagnostic passes@comma{} but @code{--check} errors or is inaudible
The failure is below Emacsvox.  Do not edit the Emacs init file.  Follow
@ref{Backend Diagnostics And Ownership} until the check produces both its tone
and speech.  A zero exit status without both audible signals is not success.
@item The audible check passes@comma{} but the isolated startup sentence is absent
Run @code{./bin/emacsvox --diagnose} again from the same checkout and shell.
Remove an unintended @code{TTS_PROGRAM} override if the report does not select
@code{omnivox}.  Then request an Emacs startup backtrace with:

@example
./bin/emacsvox -- --debug-init
@end example

An error saying @code{Could not find speech server executable} means the
backend visible during that start cannot be executed.  Correct the reported
backend path rather than adding more init-file configuration.  For an Omnivox
process error@comma{} inspect the private log directory described below.
@item The startup sentence is heard@comma{} but speech later stops
If Emacs reports that an Emacsvox speech server exited@comma{} inspect the Omnivox
session log and type @kbd{M-x tts-restart RET} after correcting the backend
problem.  Logs are below @code{$XDG_STATE_HOME/emacsvox/omnivox}@comma{} defaulting to
@code{~/.local/state/emacsvox/omnivox}.  Spoken text is omitted unless explicit
text logging is enabled.
@item The isolated launcher works@comma{} but a customized Emacs start does not
Remove or comment out the Emacsvox environment and @code{load-file} forms from
the personal init file.  This is the rollback: it leaves the checkout and the
known-good @code{./bin/emacsvox} path intact.  Re-add the forms in the order
shown in @ref{Persistent Startup}; backend selection must happen before setup
is loaded.
@end table

@node Persistent Startup
@section Persistent Startup

Configure a normal customized start only after @ref{Confirming First Speech}
passes.  Add these forms to the user's Emacs init file@comma{} replacing the example
path with the absolute path of the checkout:

@lisp
(setenv "TTS_PROGRAM" "omnivox")
(load-file "/path/to/emacsvox/lisp/emacsvox-setup.el")
@end lisp

The order is significant: Emacsvox selects and starts the speech server while
loading setup.  A shell alternative is to export the server name before
launching Emacs:

@example
export TTS_PROGRAM=omnivox
@end example

Keep the explicit @code{load-file} form for a changing source checkout.  Its
setup entry point can warn and prefer source when newer Lisp would otherwise
be hidden by stale local byte-code.  A customized startup should speak the
same acceptance sentence as the isolated launcher.  If it does not@comma{} remove or
comment out the two init forms and return to the isolated launcher before
trying another change.

Emacsvox distributions contain the @code{.el} sources.  @code{make all}
generates ignored @code{.elc} files locally in the checkout; those build
outputs are not distributed.  After an ordinary Lisp edit@comma{} run
@code{make bytecode}.  After switching branches@comma{} pulling@comma{} or changing Lisp
macros or public interfaces@comma{} run @code{make bytecode-rebuild}.  The
non-mutating @code{make bytecode-check} verifies that local byte-code is
current and was produced by the selected Emacs.

@node Building And Testing The Speech Server
@section Building And Testing The Speech Server

Speech-server launchers live in @code{servers/}.  Build them through the
repository-root targets above so the selected paths and staging rules remain
consistent.

The Omnivox smoke test and its audible success condition are in
@ref{Selecting And Checking Omnivox}.  The following protocol-level test is
only for retained direct Tcl-compatible servers.  Run one of those launchers
from the repository root@comma{} for example:

@example
./servers/espeak
@end example

Then enter:

@example
q @{This is a test.@}
d
s
@end example

The @code{q} command queues text@comma{} @code{d} dispatches it@comma{} and @code{s} stops
speech.  Type @kbd{C-d} to close the server.  Backend-specific READMEs may
provide stronger diagnostics and should take precedence over this generic
smoke test.

From Emacs@comma{} use @code{M-x tts-speak-version} to verify the selected adapter and
@code{M-x tts-restart} after changing server configuration.  Omnivox also
records per-process diagnostic logs below
@code{$XDG_STATE_HOME/emacsvox/omnivox}@comma{} defaulting to
@code{~/.local/state/emacsvox/omnivox}; spoken text is omitted unless explicit
text logging is enabled.

@node Speech Backends
@chapter Speech Backends

Emacsvox needs a speech-server process.  For a new installation@comma{} use
@strong{Omnivox}.  Omnivox is the server that Emacsvox talks to; the engine
that produces speech underneath it is platform-specific.  The normal engine
is eSpeak NG on GNU/Linux@comma{} AVSpeechSynthesizer on macOS@comma{} and WinRT
SpeechSynthesizer on Windows.

This chapter owns Emacsvox's platform recommendation and first backend smoke
test.  Omnivox owns its build internals@comma{} engine behavior@comma{} and versioned
protocols.  The maintained Omnivox source and current status are at
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox, the Omnivox repository}.

@menu
* Supported Platform Routes::    Choose the maintained route for this machine.
* Installing Omnivox From Source:: Install on GNU/Linux or macOS.
* Building Omnivox For WSL2::    Stage the Windows runtime from WSL2.
* Selecting And Checking Omnivox:: Prove speech before starting Emacsvox.
* Compatibility Speech Servers:: Understand the retained direct adapters.
* Backend Diagnostics And Ownership:: Locate failures and authoritative detail.
@end menu

@node Supported Platform Routes
@section Supported Platform Routes

The following table describes Emacsvox support@comma{} not every platform that the
Omnivox project can build.  ``Host check required'' means that the source path
is maintained but an audible @code{omnivox --check} on the user's machine
is still the acceptance test.

@multitable @columnfractions .18 .31 .51
@headitem Emacs platform
@tab Recommended route
@tab Current support evidence
@item GNU/Linux
@tab Source-built Omnivox using eSpeak NG
@tab Supported source installation; host check required. Omnivox does not currently publish a Linux archive or run a Linux audio-runtime CI job.
@item macOS on Apple silicon
@tab Source-built Omnivox using AVSpeechSynthesizer
@tab Supported source installation; Omnivox CI executes this native target@comma{} and the host check remains required.
@item macOS on Intel
@tab Source-built Omnivox using AVSpeechSynthesizer
@tab Available source installation; current Omnivox CI cross-builds this target but does not execute it. Treat the host check as required validation.
@item Emacs in WSL2 on x86-64 Windows
@tab Emacsvox-staged Windows Omnivox using WinRT
@tab Supported advanced deployment; the pinned build verifies its payload@comma{} but speech and audio must be accepted on the target Windows host.
@item Native Windows Emacs
@tab Omnivox executable on @code{exec-path}@comma{} using WinRT
@tab Preview. The executable-resolution path exists@comma{} but Emacsvox does not yet publish or fresh-machine test a complete native-Windows setup. Initial users should use the WSL2 route.
@end multitable

There is no verified public binary-download path in this guide.  Build from
source as described below.  A repository tag@comma{} a CI artifact@comma{} or an executable
without its adjacent runtime data is not a substitute for a supported install
payload.

Do not set @code{OMNIVOX_ENGINE} during initial setup.  Its empty value selects
the normal platform engine above.  Engine routing and optional Piper@comma{}
Eloquence@comma{} or DECtalk support are later customizations@comma{} not prerequisites for
first speech.

@node Installing Omnivox From Source
@section Installing Omnivox From Source

Use this route on GNU/Linux and macOS@.  It requires network access for the
initial clone@comma{} Rust toolchain@comma{} and locked dependency downloads.

Install these prerequisite classes before building:

@itemize
@item
@uref{https://rustup.rs/, rustup}; the checked-in
@code{rust-toolchain.toml} selects the supported Rust release.

@item
Python 3@comma{} a C and C++ toolchain@comma{} CMake@comma{} and Clang with libclang.  The supported
build wrapper uses these to build and stage eSpeak NG and its generated data.

@item
On GNU/Linux@comma{} @code{pkg-config} and ALSA development headers.  The ALSA
package is commonly named @code{libasound2-dev} on Debian or Ubuntu and
@code{alsa-lib-devel} on Fedora.

@item
On macOS@comma{} the Xcode command-line tools.  Install them with
@code{xcode-select --install} if they are absent.
@end itemize

Keep the two repositories beside one another; this also matches the WSL2
release layout:

@example
cd /path/containing/emacsvox
git clone https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox
cd omnivox
make build
make install
@end example

@code{make build} performs the locked release build and stages the matching
eSpeak data and third-party notices.  @code{make install} installs the
binary and those adjacent directories@comma{} normally below @code{~/.cargo/bin}.
Set @code{OMNIVOX_INSTALL_BIN} before @code{make install} only when the Cargo
binary directory is elsewhere.  Do not use a bare @code{cargo build} as the
installation procedure; it does not create the complete staged payload.

Make the install directory visible on @code{PATH} before continuing.  No
proprietary speech runtime is needed for the recommended Linux or macOS path.

@node Building Omnivox For WSL2
@section Building Omnivox For WSL2

Use this route when Emacs runs inside WSL2 and speech should play through
Windows.  Clone Omnivox beside Emacsvox as shown above.  The host must provide:

@itemize
@item
WSL2 with working @code{powershell.exe} and @code{wslpath} interoperation;

@item
Docker with Linux/AMD64 container support;

@item
Windows .NET Framework 4.7.2 or newer; and

@item
@code{curl}@comma{} @code{unzip}@comma{} and @code{sha256sum} in WSL@comma{} plus network
access for the pinned container and .NET toolchain inputs.
@end itemize

From a clean Emacsvox checkout whose sibling Omnivox checkout also has no
tracked changes@comma{} run:

@example
cd /path/to/emacsvox
make windows-omnivox
@end example

This is the reproducible release path.  It builds and verifies a pinned
Windows-GNU executable and stages a content-addressed runtime beneath
@code{servers/omnivox-bin}.  It also copies the runnable payload to a
Windows-local directory so native voice and eSpeak data discovery do not pay
the WSL filesystem cost.  Optional proprietary Eloquence and DECtalk runtimes
are not downloaded or distributed.

Maintainers testing tracked changes may use
@code{make windows-omnivox-dev}.  That target records both worktree-diff
hashes and labels the result as a development build; it is not the clean
release procedure.  See @code{servers/omnivox-release/README.org} for the
pinned toolchain@comma{} licensing@comma{} provenance@comma{} and restaging contract.

@node Selecting And Checking Omnivox
@section Selecting And Checking Omnivox

First test Omnivox without Emacs.  On GNU/Linux or macOS@comma{} run the installed
binary:

@example
omnivox --version
omnivox --check
omnivox --list-voices
@end example

Under WSL2@comma{} test the content-addressed launcher instead:

@example
./servers/omnivox --check
@end example

The check initializes the selected engine and audio output@comma{} then plays a tone
and speech.  Success ends with a message saying that everything is working if
both were heard.  Do not continue merely because the command exited zero:
hearing both signals is the backend acceptance condition.

Next select the server before loading Emacsvox:

@example
export TTS_PROGRAM=omnivox
@end example

Make that setting visible in the shell used to launch Emacs.  An
@code{export} affects only that shell and its children; placing it in a
shell startup file makes the choice persistent for later sessions.  On POSIX
systems@comma{} Emacsvox's @code{servers/omnivox} launcher finds an installed
@code{omnivox} on @code{PATH}; @code{OMNIVOX_PROGRAM} can instead name its
absolute executable.  Native Windows Emacs resolves @code{omnivox.exe}
directly through @code{exec-path}@comma{} but that route remains preview.

Inside a running Emacsvox session@comma{} @code{M-x omnivox} selects the same server
temporarily.  Use @code{M-x tts-speak-version} to identify the live adapter
and @code{M-x tts-restart} after changing server configuration.  This backend
contract stops at a proven speech server; the complete isolated Emacs start@comma{}
expected startup speech@comma{} and no-speech recovery belong to the first-use
procedure.

@node Compatibility Speech Servers
@section Compatibility Speech Servers

Emacsvox retains direct speech servers for existing installations.  They are
not the recommended first setup and are outside the initial-user platform
matrix above:

@table @code
@item espeak
The direct GNU/Linux Tcl server.  Building it with @code{make espeak}
requires Tclx at runtime@comma{} Tcl 8.6 development headers@comma{} eSpeak NG development
libraries@comma{} and a C++ compiler.  It remains useful for compatibility testing@comma{}
but new users should install Omnivox instead.
@item swiftmac
The direct Swift macOS server.  It requires Swift 5.9 or newer and is built
with @code{make swiftmac}.  It remains a compatibility alternative to
Omnivox's AVSpeechSynthesizer engine; see
@code{servers/mac-swiftmac/Readme.org}.
@item mac
The legacy Python/PyObjC macOS server.  The unset runtime selector still names
it as a compatibility default on Darwin@comma{} but it is not the recommended or
documented initial-user route.  It depends on Foundation@comma{} AppKit@comma{} and
PyObjCTools availability.
@item outloud
The direct GNU/Linux IBM TTS or Eloquence adapter.  It requires a separately
licensed compatible runtime@comma{} which Emacsvox does not distribute.  Voxin
availability is documented at @uref{https://voxin.oralux.net}.
@item dtk-soft
The direct Software DECtalk adapter.  Install its runtime separately and see
@code{servers/software-dtk/Readme.org}.  Emacsvox does not distribute licensed
speech runtimes.
@end table

The native Windows audio@comma{} standalone Eloquence@comma{} and standalone DECtalk bridges
under @code{servers/} are advanced WSL-specific compatibility paths.  Piper is
an optional Omnivox engine and model workflow@comma{} not a primary Emacsvox server.

@node Backend Diagnostics And Ownership
@section Backend Diagnostics And Ownership

Use the boundary at which speech failed to choose the next diagnostic:

@table @asis
@item @code{omnivox} is not found
Check @code{PATH}; on POSIX@comma{} an absolute @code{OMNIVOX_PROGRAM} can identify the
installed executable.  Keep its adjacent @code{espeak-ng-data} and
@code{third-party-licenses} directories with it.
@item @code{omnivox --check} reports an error or is inaudible
The failure is below Emacsvox.  Consult Omnivox's
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/README.md, build guide}@comma{}
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/STATUS.md, platform status}@comma{}
and
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/docs/DIAGNOSTICS.md, diagnostics guide}.  Do not diagnose Emacs configuration until the tone and
speech check passes.
@item The check passes but Emacsvox is silent
Confirm that @code{TTS_PROGRAM=omnivox} was present before Emacsvox loaded@comma{} then
use @code{M-x tts-speak-version}.  Under WSL2@comma{} also run
@code{make verify-windows-omnivox-runtime} and restart the speech process
after restaging.
@item A process starts and later fails
The Emacsvox POSIX/WSL launcher writes private@comma{} bounded stderr logs below
@code{$XDG_STATE_HOME/emacsvox/omnivox}@comma{} defaulting to
@code{~/.local/state/emacsvox/omnivox}.  Spoken text is excluded unless
@code{OMNIVOX_LOG_SYNTHESIS_TEXT} is explicitly enabled; enabling it can expose
passwords@comma{} messages@comma{} and document contents.
@end table

Emacsvox owns server selection@comma{} its POSIX/WSL launcher@comma{} Windows staging@comma{} and
the bundled Lisp adapter.  Omnivox owns its native builds@comma{} engines@comma{} command
line@comma{} runtime data layout@comma{} and protocol specifications.  Backend-local
READMEs own licensed-runtime and compatibility-adapter details.  Cross-link
these owners rather than copying their versioned contracts into installation
instructions.

@node Basic Usage
@chapter Basic Usage.

This chapter gives a short@comma{} task-oriented introduction to Emacsvox.
For the complete generated key reference@comma{} see
@ref{Emacsvox Keymaps,,,emacsvox-reference,Emacsvox Reference Manual}.
Use @kbd{C-h m} to inspect the modes and bindings active in the current
buffer@comma{} or @kbd{C-h k} followed by a key to ask Emacs what that key does.
These sections describe the speech-enabling extensions@comma{} but are not a
substitute for the Emacs manual.

All Emacs navigation and editing commands have been speech enabled.
Thus@comma{} moving to the next or previous word@comma{} line or paragraph results
in the text around point being spoken.  Exactly how much text is
spoken is determined by the amount by which you moved.

In addition@comma{} Emacsvox provides  basic reading functions that can be
invoked to listen to chunks of text without moving.

@menu
* Overview::                     Basic Overview.
* Working::                      Working in Emacsvox.
* Reading::                      Reading chunks of information.
* Speech System::                Controlling Speech Output.
* Voice-lock::                   Audio Formatting.
* Status Information::           Speaking Status Information.
@end menu

@node Overview
@section Emacsvox Overview

Emacsvox provides a small number of core services around which the
remainder of the audio interface is constructed.  These essential
features of the software are briefly outlined in the following
paragraphs; the commands by which they can be controlled will be
described later in the manual.

Apart from providing a fluent spoken interface to all of Emacs' basic
editing functions@comma{} Emacsvox also includes software modules which add
speech feedback to a range of applications that can be run from within
Emacs. In this sense@comma{} Emacsvox amounts to much more than a talking
text editor; indeed@comma{} it can more aptly be characterized as a true
``audio desktop''@comma{} in which speech is treated as a first-class output modality.

Emacsvox implements a special minor mode@comma{} known as ``voice lock mode''
(@pxref{Voice-lock}) which uses distinct speech characteristics to
provide aural highlighting of specific textual constructs@comma{} such as
comments in program code@comma{} quoted strings and reserved words @xref{Voice Lock}. This facility is further extended in the supported @code{EWW} Web
browser@comma{} where semantic and structural distinctions captured by HTML markup
can be communicated efficiently; see @ref{Web Browsing}.

It is often desirable to exercise control over the pronunciation of a
word (E.G@. a technical term or a reserved word in a programming
language) within specific contexts. Emacsvox maintains pronunciation
dictionaries for this purpose@comma{} which may be customized by the
user. Moreover@comma{} individual dictionaries can be activated selectively@comma{}
depending for example on the current major mode or the name of the
file which is being visited; see
@ref{emacsvox-pronounce,,,emacsvox-reference,Emacsvox Reference Manual}.

In addition to spoken feedback@comma{} Emacsvox can generate ``auditory
icons''  @MDash{} short sound cues which alert the user to significant
events@comma{} for example the opening or deletion of a file@comma{} the completion
of an action@comma{} the arrival of an electronic mail message or the
creation of a completion buffer. Sound cues act as a supplement to the
spoken interface@comma{} and are especially valuable to the experienced user
in facilitating rapid interaction. Note that in order to support
auditory icons@comma{} the computer must be equipped with sound hardware for
which the operating system has been correctly configured; see
@ref{emacsvox-sounds,,,emacsvox-reference,Emacsvox Reference Manual}.

@node Working
@section Using Emacs Buffers

While typing in an Emacs buffer@comma{} hitting space speaks the recently
typed word.  I use completion all the time; so Emacsvox will speak
the completion just inserted as well as the next possible completion.
In Emacs@comma{} use @kbd{M-x load-library @key{RETURN} completion @key{RETURN}} for loading the completion package.

The standard Emacs prompting functions have also been speech-enabled.
Emacs prompts with available lists of completions in response to partial
input wherever appropriate   @MDash{} all forms of completion provide speech
feedback.

In addition@comma{} Emacsvox provides a number of commands for reading
portions of the current buffer@comma{} getting status information@comma{} and
modifying Emacsvox's state.

All of the commands are documented in the subsequent sections.
They can be classified into types:

@itemize
@item
Emacsvox commands for listening to chunks of information.  Most of
these command names start with @code{emacsvox-speak-}.  Their default
bindings are grouped under the main Emacsvox prefix @kbd{C-e}@footnote{The prefix is inherited from Emacspeak@comma{} where C-e was mnemonic for the project name.}.  The starter bindings are introduced in @ref{Reading};
the live Help system and generated key reference remain authoritative
when bindings are customized.

@item
The second category manipulates the speech engine.  These commands
normally start with @code{tts-} and are grouped under @kbd{C-e d}
@footnote{Historically@comma{} d was mnemonic for DECtalk; the prefix now contains engine-independent speech controls.}.
@end itemize

Emacs and Emacsvox both expose their current commands through Emacs Help.
