@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@cindex Introduction

Emacsvox is an Emacs 31-or-newer audio desktop derived from Emacspeak.  It is
not a pixel-oriented screen reader.  It runs inside Emacs@comma{} where commands@comma{}
buffers@comma{} text properties@comma{} faces@comma{} modes@comma{} and application state are available as
structured information.  That context lets Emacsvox present the meaning and
result of an interaction rather than merely reading the visible display.

Application integrations adapt interactive commands and changing buffers to
produce spoken text@comma{} voice changes@comma{} auditory icons@comma{} tones@comma{} pauses@comma{} and spatial
feedback.  The core speech layer normalizes text and formatting and sends it
to the selected speech adapter.  Established direct speech paths remain
available for compatibility@comma{} while migrated integrations can submit a complete
Aural Presentation transaction.

@section Aural Presentation

Aural Presentation separates semantic facts from their audible rendering.
An integration records what an object or event means; providers and rules
choose voices and actions; a planner and compiler produce a concrete ordered
presentation.  The fixed internal baseline preserves established Emacsvox
feedback.  Users can compose Presentation Options@comma{} profiles@comma{} palettes@comma{} sound
packs@comma{} spatial settings@comma{} and scoped overrides without replacing that baseline.

Open Aural Home with @kbd{C-e H} or
@code{M-x emacsvox-aural}.  Use @kbd{C-e E} to explain the presentation at
point.  The generated author and lifecycle reference is
@code{etc/aural-presentation-reference.org} in the source tree.

@section Speech Adapters And Omnivox

Emacsvox retains standalone speech servers for engines such as eSpeak@comma{}
Eloquence@comma{} DECtalk@comma{} and SwiftMac.  Omnivox is the recommended multi-engine
server for new GNU/Linux@comma{} macOS@comma{} and WSL2 installations.  It negotiates
capabilities@comma{} reports live engine and physical-voice inventory@comma{} routes portable
logical voices@comma{} and accepts structured presentation timelines.  Keyed
replaceable timelines cancel only older work in the same replacement domain;
ordered and urgent work are not placed behind the replaceable coalescing
window.

@xref{Speech Backends}@comma{} for Emacsvox's platform status@comma{} Omnivox installation@comma{}
selection@comma{} smoke test@comma{} and compatibility policy.  The Emacsvox
@code{Readme.org} documents advanced portable voice routing.  Omnivox owns the
detailed control@comma{} helper@comma{} and presentation-timeline protocol contracts in its
own repository.  The legacy line protocol remains supported for standalone
and older speech servers; @xref{TTS Servers} explains the boundary between
those protocol families.

@section Documentation Scope

This manual includes generated command documentation as well as inherited
Emacspeak essays and application guides.  Historical chapters are useful for
design context but may describe software and Web services from their original
period.  Current setup starts with @xref{Installation}; rapidly changing
features are documented in @code{Readme.org}@comma{} @code{etc/NEWS}@comma{} and the generated
Aural Presentation reference.

@node Audio Desktop
@chapter The Emacsvox Audio Desktop.

@cindex Audio Desktop

This chapter describes the Emacsvox audio desktop and gives tips and
tricks for making use of many of Emacs' powerful features.

The desktop is the work area where you organize the tools of your
trade and the information objects relevant to your current activities.
In the conventional world of visual @acronym{GUI,Graphical User Interface}-based computing@comma{} these tools and information objects
manifest themselves as a collection of icons organized in a
two-dimensional work-area  @MDash{} this organization is designed to
place frequently used objects within easy reach.

Notice that organizing one's work area in terms of visual icons
arranged in a two-dimensional area where such an organization is
optimized for the available ``conversational gestures'' of pointing and
clicking is an artifact of visual interaction.

In the spirit of a truly speech-enabled application@comma{} Emacsvox does
not simply provide you spoken access to a particular presentation of
your work environment that was initially designed with the ``sign
language'' of visual interaction in mind.  Instead@comma{} Emacsvox enables
you to work with documents and other information objects in a manner
that is optimized to aural@comma{} eyes-free interaction.  A necessary
consequence of this setup is that users accustomed to the purely
visual manifestation of today's electronic desktop do not immediately
perceive the Emacsvox environment as an electronic desktop.  This
section of the manual hopes to introduce you to a work-style that
encourages a different perspective on how one interacts with the
computer in performing day-to-day computing tasks.

The end result in my case has been a marked increase in personal
productivity.

@menu
* Desktop Objects::              Desktop Objects
* Object Oriented Desktop::      Object Oriented Desktop
* Context-Sensitive Interaction:: Context-Sensitive Interaction
@end menu

@node Desktop Objects
@section Objects Making Up The Emacsvox Desktop

@cindex Desktop Objects

A ``buffer'' is the basic building block of  the
Emacsvox desktop.  Any information presented by Emacs is placed in a
``buffer''.  For example@comma{} when perusing this manual within Emacs@comma{} the
``file'' containing the documentation is presented in a ``buffer''.  All
information objects such as @acronym{WWW} pages@comma{} email messages@comma{} output from
user interaction with command-line shells etc.@comma{} are presented by Emacs
in individual ``buffers''.

Buffers provide a base level of user interaction; Emacs derives its
power by allowing applications to specialize buffers to enable
specific types of user-interaction that is optimized for a specific
class of information.

@node Object Oriented Desktop
@section An Object-Oriented Desktop

@cindex Object-Oriented Desktop

The basic ``buffer object'' can be specialized by Emacs applications
to provide optimal interaction.  This kind of specialization makes the
Emacs environment an object-oriented environment; thus@comma{} the basic
conversational gesture of ``move to the next statement'' can be
assigned behavior that is appropriate to the content that the user is
currently navigating.  As an example of such specialization@comma{} Emacs
provides ``specialized modes'' for working with English text@comma{}
programming languages@comma{} markup source e.g. @acronym{HTML,Hypertext Markup Language} or @LaTeX{} documents and so on.

@node Context-Sensitive Interaction
@section Emacsvox Specializes Aural Interaction

@cindex Context-Sensitive Interaction

The content-specific user interaction described above is a very
powerful feature of Emacs@comma{} and this is where Emacsvox derives its
power.  Traditionally@comma{} the ability to create buffers specialized for
working with specific content-types has been used by the Emacs
community to develop versatile programming environments@comma{} messaging
applications such as mail and news readers@comma{} and authoring
environments.  The clean design present in all of these Emacs
extensions in terms of separating application functionality from the
user-interface@comma{} combined with the availability of the entire source
code making up these packages under the open-source model has laid the
ground-work for developing Emacsvox as a versatile aural counterpart
to the product of years of software engineering that has been invested
by the Emacs community.  In short@comma{} Emacsvox would not exist in its
present shape or form without this prior effort.

@subsection Audio Formatted Output

@cindex Audio Formatting

Emacsvox takes advantage of the content-specific knowledge available
within specialized buffers to produce ``audio formatted'' output
designed to optimize user interaction.  A basic consequence of the
above is ``voice locking'' in specialized modes; a more interesting
consequence is the implementation of Aural Cascading Style Sheets
(@acronym{ACSS}) in conjunction with the Emacs @acronym{EWW,Emacs Web Wowser} and @acronym{W3} Web browsers.

@subsection Structured Navigation:

@cindex Structured Navigation

Emacsvox also exploits content-specific knowledge to provide
structured navigation of different types of electronic content.  In
many cases@comma{} such structured navigation is an extension of what Emacs
provides by default; in other cases@comma{} Emacsvox implements the
necessary extensions to provide the level of structural navigation
needed to work efficiently in an eyes-free environment.

Notable among such structured navigation is Emacs' powerful outline
feature.

An example of content-sensitive navigation is provided by the @code{imenu}
package which dynamically creates a ``table of contents'' based on the
content that is being displayed in a given buffer.  Emacs
@dfn{Selective Display} lets one easily hide and expose portions of a
buffer based on indentation  @MDash{} this feature can be used to
advantage when working with program source code.

@subsection Navigating The Desktop

@cindex Desktop Navigation

In addition to navigating individual information objects@comma{} the
Emacsvox environment provides speech-enabled navigation of the
various buffers that are currently open on the Emacsvox desktop via
Emacs' built-in list-buffers feature.  Emacs' dired  @MDash{} directory
editor  @MDash{} for browsing the file system@comma{} along with the new
speedbar package that combines features from dired and imenu round off
the suite of navigational tools.

@subsection Everything Is Searchable:

@cindex Searching

@cindex Finding

Emacs derives one final advantage from using buffers as the basic
building block for the entire desktop.  Every Emacs buffer is
searchable via a uniform and powerful search interface.  Emacs'
incremental search works efficiently and consistently to enable you
locate ``objects'' of interest either within a given document or to
locate a given object from among the various objects that are
currently open on the Emacsvox desktop.  This is @b{very powerful}
 @MDash{} where a @acronym{GUI,Graphical User Interface} user is
typically limited to quickly locating an object from a relatively
small collection  @MDash{} the size of the collection being a direct
function of available display real-estate  @MDash{} the Emacsvox user
can typically work with a far larger collection of objects.  This is
well-suited to the eyes-free environment@comma{} where display real-estate
has no meaning; so bringing up a list of currently open buffers and
performing an incremental search to locate a specific buffer is just
as efficient independent of whether you have a few dozen or a few
hundred buffers open.

To illustrate the above@comma{} my typical working Emacs session lasts
between two and three weeks  @MDash{} over that time I typically
accumulate several hundred open buffers holding a large variety of
content ranging from program source code to email messages and
@acronym{WWW} pages.

Ubiquitous search in the eyes-free environment is critical  @MDash{} as
a comparison@comma{} when using a conventional@comma{} purely visual WWW browser@comma{}
users have no means of easily ``searching'' for say the ``submit''
button on a WWW page.  This inability is a minor annoyance in visual
interaction@comma{} and the typical mouse-enabled user @b{never} uses the
find dialog to find a submit button  @MDash{} it is simply more
efficient to point at the submit button given the eye's ability to
quickly scan the two-dimensional display.  This luxury is absent in an
eyes-free environment; as a consequence@comma{} blind users confronted by the
combination of a visual interface and screen-reader are typically
limited to either tabbing through all the controls on a @acronym{WWW}
page@comma{} or using the sub-optimal find dialog.

@node Voice Lock
@chapter Voice Lock

See
@uref{http://tvraman.github.io/emacspeak/blog/voice-lock-refreshed.html}
for a high-level overview of how Emacsvox Voice-Lock has evolved over
the years.

@enumerate
@item
Emacsvox defines a number of voice overlays such as
@code{voice-bolden}@comma{} and @code{voice-lighten} that can be applied to a
given voice to change what it sounds like.

@item
Voice overlays are defined in terms of Aural CSS
(@uref{http://www.w3.org/tr/css2/aural.html, ACSS}) to keep them
independent of a specific TTS engine.

@item
For each such overlay there is a corresponding
@code{<OVERLAY-NAME>-settings} variable that can be customized via
custom.

@item
The numbers in @code{voice-bolden-settings} as an example:
@end enumerate

@multitable @columnfractions 0.5 0.5
@item Setting
@tab Value
@item family
@tab nil
@item average-pitch
@tab 1
@item pitch-range
@tab 6
@item stress
@tab 6
@item richness
@tab nil
@item punctuation
@tab nil
@end multitable

Unset values (@code{nil}) show up as ``unspecified'' in the customize
interface.

@enumerate
@item
Do not directly customize @code{voice-bolden} and friends@comma{} instead
customize the corresponding @code{voice-bolden-settings}@comma{} since that
ensures that all voices that are defined in terms of
@code{voice-bolden} get correctly updated.

@item
Discovering what to customize:
@end enumerate

Command @code{emacsvox-show-personality-at-point} (bound by default
to C-e M-v) will show you the value of properties personality and
face at point.  Describe-variable on these names should tell you
what to customize; so as an example:

Put point on a comment line@comma{} and hit @kbd{C-e M-v}: you will hear

@example
Personality emacsvox-voice-lock-comment-personality
Face font-lock-comment-delimiter-face
@end example

Describe-variable of @code{emacsvox-voice-lock-comment-personality} gives:

@example
emacsvox-voice-lock-comment-personality's value is acss-p0-s0-all

Documentation: Personality used for font-lock-comment-face This
personality uses voice-monotone whose effect can be changed globally
by customizing voice-monotone-settings.
@end example

@menu
* How It All Works.::
* What this gives.::
@end menu

@node How It All Works.
@section How It All Works

Here is a brief explanation of the connection between
@code{voice-bolden} and its associated @code{voice-bolden-settings}.

@enumerate
@item
Voice settings are initially in @code{voice-bolden-settings} which is
a list of numbers.

@item
That list of numbers needs to be translated to appropriate
device-specific codes to send to the TTS engine.

@item
You do not want to do this translation @strong{each} time you
speak something.

@item
So when @code{voice-bolden} is defined@comma{} the definition happens in
two steps:

@itemize
@item
The list of settings is stored away in
@code{voice-bolden-settings}@comma{}

@item
A corresponding voice-name is generated   @MDash{}
@code{acss-a<n>-p<n>-r<n>-s<n>} and the corresponding control codes to
send to the device are stored away in a hash-table keyed by the above symbol.

@item
Finally@comma{} @code{voice-bolden} is assigned the above symbol.
@end itemize
@end enumerate

@node What this gives.
@section What this gives

@enumerate
@item
The ability to customize the voice via custom by editing
the list of numbers in @code{voice-bolden-settings}

@item
When that list is edited@comma{} @code{voice-bolden} is arranged to be
updated automatically.
@end enumerate

The following additional commands from the
@ref{emacsvox-wizards,,,emacsvox-reference,Emacsvox Reference Manual} module
are useful when designing aural styles.

@enumerate
@item
@code{emacsvox-wizards-generate-voice-sampler}

Generate a buffer containing text that demonstrates the effect of
various aural settings.

@item
@code{emacsvox-wizards-voice-sampler}

Applied specified aural style to text in current region.
@end enumerate

@node Using Online Help
@chapter Using Online Help With Emacsvox.

@cindex Online Help

Emacs provides an extensive online help system for helping you learn
about various aspects of using Emacs.  Emacsvox provides online help
for its various extensions using this same help system.  This chapter
explains how to use the online help facilities in order to empower you
in discovering powerful and versatile working techniques that will
make you more and more productive in your day to day computing.

The online help options are accessed via the @kbd{C-h} prefix key@comma{}
which must be followed by an additional letter or control character to
designate the kind of help desired.  For example@comma{} @kbd{C-h t}
@code{help-with-tutorial} visits the Emacs tutorial in a new buffer;
@kbd{C-h i} @code{info} enters the Info documentation system@comma{} from
which you can read Texinfo manuals that have been installed on your
system@comma{} including the Emacs and Emacsvox documentation; and @kbd{C-h k} @code{describe-key} provides a description of the Emacs function
which is bound to the next key that you type.  For learning about the
various options that are available via the @kbd{C-h} mechanism
described above@comma{} view the online help for command @code{help-for-help}
bound to @kbd{C-h C-h}  @MDash{} using what has been described so far@comma{}
you would achieve this by pressing @kbd{C-h k} followed by @kbd{C-h C-h}.

Emacsvox users should note that online help is typically displayed in
a separate Emacs window.  Where it makes sense to do so@comma{} Emacsvox
will automatically speak the displayed help.  Once you've asked for
help@comma{} you can hear the displayed documentation as many times as you
wish using Emacsvox command @code{emacsvox-speak-help} bound to
@kbd{C-e h}.  If you want to move through the displayed help a line at
a time@comma{} switch to the buffer where the help is displayed  @MDash{} the
buffer is called @code{*Help*}.

Often@comma{} in adding an auditory interface to an Emacs extension@comma{} such as
a web browser or mail reader@comma{} Emacsvox defines additional commands
and key bindings which enhance the functionality of the spoken
feedback provided by the application.  This manual does not purport to
document all such commands.  It is important@comma{} therefore@comma{} when learning
to use the various Emacs extensions which comprise the `audio desktop'
(@pxref{Audio Desktop}) that you take advantage of online help to
obtain details of any context-specific features provided by Emacsvox.
The following two commands are of particular importance in this
regard:

@itemize
@item
@kbd{C-h m} @code{describe-mode} explains which major and minor modes
are currently in effect@comma{} and lists the commands and key bindings
associated with them.

@item
@kbd{C-h b} @code{describe-bindings} lists all of the key bindings
which are currently defined.
@end itemize

For example@comma{} after entering Dired or EWW@comma{} use @kbd{C-h m} to hear the
mode-specific commands and every active minor mode.  Use @kbd{C-h b} when you
need the exact effective bindings@comma{} including local package maps and personal
configuration.  These live descriptions are more reliable than copying a
large binding table into a tutorial.

Emacsvox supplements the online help facilities available within
Emacs by defining several commands of its own@comma{} as follows:

@itemize
@item
@kbd{C-h C-e} @code{emacsvox-describe-emacsvox} presents a brief Emacsvox
overview.

@item
@kbd{C-e N} @code{emacsvox-view-emacsvox-news} opens the versioned Emacsvox
news@comma{} and @kbd{C-e T} @code{emacsvox-view-emacsvox-tips} opens the productivity
tips.

@item
@kbd{C-h C-l} @code{emacsvox-learn-emacs} enters a loop in which the function
of every key that you type is spoken; leave it with @kbd{C-g}.

@item
@kbd{C-e H} @code{emacsvox-aural} opens Aural Home.  From an ordinary buffer@comma{}
@kbd{C-e E} @code{emacsvox-aural-explain-presentation} explains why the object
at point received its voices and auditory actions.
@end itemize

@node Emacs Packages
@chapter Applications And Integrations

@cindex applications

@cindex integrations

@cindex packages, speech support

Emacsvox prepares speech support for many Emacs modes as their libraries are
loaded.  Start the application normally; when its buffer appears@comma{} use
@kbd{C-h m} for the active modes and their commands@comma{} @kbd{C-h b} for the
effective bindings@comma{} and @kbd{C-h k} followed by a key to identify one binding.
The generated
@ref{Emacsvox Commands And Options,,,emacsvox-reference,Emacsvox Reference Manual} records the public Emacsvox commands and options.

This chapter is a curated starting point@comma{} not a promise that every module in
the generated reference has a maintained task guide.  Emacs itself or a
third-party package owns the application's general operation; Emacsvox owns
the additional speech feedback.  Consult both sets of help when diagnosing a
problem.

Earlier Emacspeak surveys@comma{} including their W3@comma{} Dismal@comma{} and scanner/OCR setup@comma{}
are preserved as historical material in
@ref{Emacs Packages,Historical Application Survey,,emacsvox-heritage,Emacspeak Heritage}.  They are not current setup instructions.

